## Context

The last step of 0942: once every consumer resolves at point of use, remove the
credential-loading half of `bash-env.sh` so nothing is resident any more.

Blocked by 0943 and 0944, its real prerequisites -- NOT by the 0942 tracker.
Landing this first would break every consumer that still reads the ambient
environment.

What remains of `bash-env.sh` afterwards is the project `.env` strict-parse and
the protected-name refusal, which are worth keeping and are independently
tested. The 0939 xtrace guard becomes vestigial for credentials but still
covers whatever the strict-parse assigns; decide at implementation time whether
to keep it, and say which in the commit message.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `bash-env.sh` no longer sources or exports any keystore provider file
- [ ] `KEYS=` handling is REMOVED OUTRIGHT -- decided by the author 2026-09-16.
      No no-op, no deprecation shim: a residual inert `KEYS=` line would read
      as configuration that still does something. The `.env` line goes too.
- [ ] `bash -x -c ':'` exposes no credential value (names-only probe, as in 0939)
- [ ] `/proc/<pid>/environ` of a fresh child carries no credential
- [ ] Every existing bash-env suite is updated or retired deliberately, none
      left green-while-testing-nothing
- [ ] 0360 re-read against the result and closed or re-scoped with a reason
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Imagine + blind-spot pass 2026-09-16

**The boundary is clean in code, messy in prose.** Removable, job (a), three
contiguous blocks totalling ~240 lines: 196-199 (sourcing the trusted
`~/.claude/.env`, which on this host holds nothing but `KEYS=` and comments, so
it exists solely for job a), 201-219 (`_be_harness_keys` / `_be_explain`
bookkeeping), 296-371 (the 0361 `KEYS_EXPLAIN` drop-diagnostic), 372-510 (the
provider loop). Staying, job (b): 220-294 (project `.env` strict parse), 181-196
(`_be_is_protected_name`, called from both jobs but sharing no logic beyond the
predicate), 176-179 + 514-520 (the xtrace guard). The HEADER (lines 1-131) is
the real editing cost: ~87 lines are pure KEYS documentation and the "two .env
sources with different trust levels" framing collapses to one source. Budget for
it rather than treating this as a deletion.

**Catch the leftover:** `KEYS_EXPLAIN` sits in the protected-name denylist
(line ~190) only because of the removed 0361 diagnostic. Leaving it is precisely
the "residual inert thing that reads as still doing something" this ticket
forbids for `KEYS=`.

**The 0939 xtrace guard is NOT vestigial — keep it.** This corrects the ticket's
own earlier framing. The guard brackets the whole body including the strict-parse
loop's `export "$_be_key=$_be_val"` (line 289), which exports an already-expanded
literal from the UNTRUSTED project `.env`. Nothing in the protected-name refusal
stops a project declaring `DATABASE_PASSWORD=`; the loop exports it and `bash -x`
would trace it. Same defect class as 0939, different source of the value. Rewrite
the guard's comment to name what it now protects, and say so in the commit
message.

**Test corpus, classified.** Delete outright (pure job a): `keys_precedence`
(271 l.), `keys_protect_critical_names` (250 l.), `keys_selection_explicit`
(335 l.), `keys_selection` (198 l.). Untouched (structure-agnostic):
`tested_via_subprocess`, `test_bash_tests_are_hermetic`. **MIXED — edit, do not
delete or leave:** `project_env_parse` (drop case 5, the trusted-source
regression, and case 7b, which goes vacuous once nothing reads `$KEYS`);
`robustness` (drop case 2, CRLF on the KEYS line); `xtrace_silent` (cases 1, 2
and 3 test paths that will not exist — **and the suite goes green testing
nothing unless a new sentinel case proves the strict-parse loop does not leak a
project-`.env` value under `-x`**). That last one is this ticket's own exit
criterion biting its own newest test.

**Consumer enumeration verified independently**, not taken from the tracker:
`peer_review.py:87,94` and `skills/update-publist/SKILL.md:74-85` are still
ambient and are the two blockers. `seat-runner.sh` is genuinely already covered
by 0393 — its value comes from `--credential-env` resolved at point of use, not
from residency. No fourth consumer exists in this repo.

**Blind-spot finding, and its correction.** The pass flagged
`scripts/pipeline_keystore.py` in consuming projects (named in this file's own
header at line 78) as an out-of-repo reader of `$KEYS` that could break
silently. Checked at the call site rather than from the comment:
`climate-finance-het/scripts/pipeline_loaders.py:55` calls
`load_dotenv(BASE_DIR/.env)` and only then `apply_keys_selection()` at line 63,
so that repo populates `os.environ["KEYS"]` itself via python-dotenv and never
depends on `bash-env.sh`. **No breakage.** What remains is divergence: after
this ticket the harness abandons a mechanism a consuming project still
implements, and this file's line-78 comment claiming the two paths "agree"
becomes false. Fix the comment here; the other repo's copy is its own business.

**Author's decision, 2026-09-16:** `KEYS` is added to the protected-name
denylist, so a project `.env` cannot export it at all. Chosen over letting the
strict-parse pass it through as an ordinary string. Safe as verified above,
because python-dotenv is unaffected by this file's refusal list.

**Not a monster ticket:** ≤8 files, one sign-off unit, dependency chain explicit
in `Blocked-by` rather than hidden. The risk here is sequencing, not scope: do
not dispatch until 0943 and 0944 are MERGED, verified in the tree rather than by
the existence of their tickets.

## Exit criteria (added 2026-09-16)
- [ ] `KEYS` added to `_be_is_protected_name`, so a project `.env` cannot export
      it; a test proves the refusal fires
- [ ] `KEYS_EXPLAIN` removed from the denylist with its comment
- [ ] The xtrace guard is KEPT, its comment rewritten to name project-`.env`
      values, and a new sentinel case proves the strict-parse loop does not leak
      one under `-x`
- [ ] The line-78 comment about the Python apply path agreeing is corrected
- [ ] Each MIXED suite edited case-by-case, none left green-while-testing-nothing

## Plan 2026-09-16 (wave 2 — blockers verified merged in the tree)

Verified before planning: both blockers are on `origin/main` (0943 as PR #942,
0944 as PR #941), `peer_review.py` and `update-publist` no longer read the
ambient environment, and `scripts/bash-env.sh` is byte-unchanged since the
Imagine pass, so its line ranges still hold. `Blocked-by` is empty in the tree,
not merely in the header.

**Actions on `scripts/bash-env.sh`.** Delete outright and contiguous: 196-199
(sourcing the trusted `~/.claude/.env`), 201-219 (`_be_harness_keys` /
`_be_explain` bookkeeping), 296-371 (the 0361 drop-diagnostic), 372-510 (the
provider loop). Keep 220-294 (project `.env` strict parse), 181-194 (the
predicate), 159-179 + 511-520 (the xtrace guard).

In `_be_is_protected_name`: ADD `KEYS` to the critical-set arm beside `PATH` and
`BASH_ENV`, not to the `*GUARD_*|_be_*` structural arm — it is a literal name,
not a namespace. REMOVE `KEYS_EXPLAIN|`, whose only reason to be protected is
the diagnostic being deleted; leaving it is exactly the inert residue this
ticket forbids for `KEYS=` itself.

The header (1-131) is the real editing cost, not the deletion. Keep 1-8 and
37-44 — they describe job (b), which survives. Delete 45-123 wholesale. Collapse
the "two .env sources with different trust levels" framing to one. And the
line-78 claim that the Python apply path "agrees" must be CORRECTED, not
silently dropped with its block: `climate-finance-het` keeps its own
`pipeline_keystore.py` via python-dotenv, and this repo is abandoning the
mechanism rather than agreeing with it.

**Test corpus.** The Imagine pass's classification was checked file by file and
holds everywhere. Delete entire: `keys_precedence` (271 l.),
`keys_protect_critical_names` (250), `keys_selection_explicit` (335),
`keys_selection` (198). Untouched: `tested_via_subprocess`,
`test_bash_tests_are_hermetic` — **except** that the latter cites
`test_bash_env_keys_selection_explicit.sh` by name at line 13 as a worked
example, and that file is being deleted. Repoint the citation; a one-line touch.

Three corrections the plan pass made to the Imagine classification, each worth
more than the classification itself:

- **`xtrace_silent` case 0 must go too.** The Imagine pass named cases 1, 2, 3.
  Case 0 is their positive control and has nothing left to control for once they
  are gone. A control without a subject is worse than no control.
- **`project_env_parse` case 5 does not go vacuous, it goes WRONG.** It asserts
  the trusted `~/.claude/.env` still gets full source behaviour. After this
  ticket that assertion is actively false against the correct implementation.
  Delete, do not weaken.
- **`project_env_parse` case 7b must be INVERTED, not deleted.** It asserts
  `KEYS=` is not a protected name and parses as a plain value. Under the new
  denylist that is backwards. Replace with `_be0345_case KEYS someprovider`
  proving the refusal fires — and that is its natural home, since the suite that
  would otherwise own it is deleted wholesale. This satisfies the exit criterion
  "a test proves the refusal fires".

Also: `robustness` drops case 2 (CRLF on the `KEYS=` line); case 1 already covers
CR stripping generically. Delete its now-orphaned `FHOME`/`fakeprov.env` fixture
with it rather than leaving a foundling.

**The replacement xtrace case.** Fixture: a project `.env` in `$TMP` holding one
recognisable fake `SENTINEL_PROJECT`. Assert `bash -x -c ':'` with
`BASH_ENV=$LOADER` and cwd `$TMP` produces no trace containing the sentinel —
this exercises the strict-parse loop's `export "$_be_key=$_be_val"` (line 287),
the surviving exposure the guard now protects. Two controls, both required:
a hand-authored unguarded script of the SAME SHAPE (`export "$K=$V"` on an
already-expanded string, no `set +x` bracket) that must leak, proving the grep
can see this shape; and a non-vacuity check that the sentinel really loaded,
since "absent from the trace" otherwise cannot distinguish a working guard from
a project `.env` that was never read.

**Every case has a named killing mutation.** New sentinel case: narrow or remove
the `set +x`/`set -x` bracket. Non-vacuity control: comment out line 287, or
break `PWD/.env` detection. Case 4: delete the final restore line or the capture
at 177. Case 5: make the restore unconditional. `_be0345_case KEYS`: remove
`KEYS` from the denylist. `robustness` case 1: delete the CR-strip at 246 — the
very mutation this repo was blind to once already.

One honest limit, stated rather than papered over: the new positive control
cannot be killed by any mutation of `bash-env.sh`, because it does not invoke
it. Its only failure mode is a grep or regex bug in the harness — which is
exactly what a positive control exists to catch.

**Scope: `REVIEWERS_KEYSTORE` stays OUT.** The round-3 red team proved an
ambient variable naming a file that will be SOURCED is a real hole, and
`reviewers.sh:33` has that shape. But a general infra denylist (`PATH`,
`BASH_ENV`, `LD_PRELOAD`) cannot enumerate every consumer's private override knob
without becoming a dumping ground, the verification burden is a different call
graph, and the durable fix belongs in `reviewers.sh` itself with the denylist as
defence in depth. Recorded in the wave follow-up ticket instead.

**Risks.** `rules/coding-bash.md:108-110` states that BASH_ENV re-runs the loader
"including its credential selection" — false the moment this lands, and not in
this ticket's file budget; fast follow-up. `scripts/trace-path-scan.py:57` and
`skills/verify-adherence/SKILL.md:196` treat this file as secret-bearing by
identity; still a reasonable heuristic, imprecise justification. And the memory
notes describing the mechanism go stale — flag in the commit message so a later
sweep catches them.

**0360 re-read:** recommend closing as SUPERSEDED rather than re-scoped. It
decided how a project `KEYS=` and a harness `KEYS=` interact; after this ticket
neither exists, so there is nothing left to re-scope it to.
